CAT = "cusEconomy"; SEG = "ECO"
Q = [
 ('Under trade rules of origin, which of these counts as goods "wholly obtained" in a country?', 'Crops harvested there', ['Cars assembled from kits', 'Imported goods repacked', 'Goods relabelled there'], 'Wholly obtained goods are grown, mined or born in the country. Repacking or relabelling imports does not give them local origin, so they do not qualify for duty-free treatment within a trade area.'),
 ('A customs union differs from a free trade area because its members also share:', 'A common external tariff', ['A single currency', 'One central bank', 'A joint army'], 'In a free trade area each member keeps its own tariff on outsiders. A customs union adds one common external tariff; a shared currency belongs to a monetary union.'),
 ('Under the ECOWAS Trade Liberalisation Scheme, approved goods made in one member state enter another member state:', 'Free of import duty', ['At half the usual duty', 'Only through seaports', 'Only with a quota'], 'The scheme lets approved goods originating in the region move between member states without import duty, though they must still be declared.'),
 ('The ECOWAS Common External Tariff is charged on goods that come:', 'From outside the region', ['From one member state', 'Into free trade zones', 'By air freight only'], 'A common external tariff means every member charges the same duty rates on imports from non-members. Trade between members falls under the liberalisation scheme instead.'),
 ('The secretariat of the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA) is in:', 'Accra', ['Abuja', 'Addis Ababa', 'Nairobi'], 'The AfCFTA secretariat is in Accra, Ghana. Addis Ababa is the seat of the African Union Commission.'),
 ('Which of these countries uses the West African CFA franc?', 'Benin', ['Cameroon', 'Ghana', 'Sierra Leone'], 'Benin uses the West African CFA franc. Cameroon uses the Central African CFA franc, Ghana the cedi and Sierra Leone the leone.'),
 ('In which year did the naira replace the Nigerian pound?', '1973', ['1960', '1966', '1980'], 'The naira and kobo were introduced on 1 January 1973, replacing the pound, shillings and pence in use since before independence.'),
 ("Which body issues the naira and runs Nigeria's monetary policy?", 'Central Bank of Nigeria', ['Federal Ministry of Finance', 'Nigerian Stock Exchange', 'Bureau of Statistics'], 'The Central Bank of Nigeria issues the currency and sets monetary policy. The Ministry of Finance runs fiscal policy: taxes and spending.'),
 ("The balance of trade is the difference between a country's:", 'Visible exports and imports', ['Government income and spending', 'Savings and investment', 'Debts and its reserves'], 'The balance of trade compares exports and imports of goods (visible trade). The wider balance of payments adds services and capital flows.'),
 ("When the value of a country's exports is greater than the value of its imports, it has a:", 'Trade surplus', ['Trade deficit', 'Budget surplus', 'Capital deficit'], 'Exports above imports give a favourable balance, a trade surplus. A budget surplus is about government income and spending.'),
 ('A common reason for a government to place a tariff on imports is to:', 'Protect local industries', ['Lower prices for buyers', 'Encourage more imports', 'Raise the value of exports'], 'A tariff makes imports dearer, giving local producers, especially young or infant industries, room to grow. It usually raises prices for buyers.'),
 ('A limit on the quantity of a good that may be imported is called:', 'A quota', ['A tariff', 'A subsidy', 'A levy'], 'A quota caps the amount; a tariff taxes it but allows any amount.'),
 ('A complete government ban on trade in a good, or with a country, is called:', 'An embargo', ['A quota', 'A countervailing duty', 'A tariff'], 'An embargo stops the trade completely. A quota limits it, and a tariff or countervailing duty taxes it.'),
 ('Selling goods abroad at a price below their normal price in the home market is called:', 'Dumping', ['Smuggling', 'Hoarding', 'Bartering'], 'Dumping can harm producers in the importing country, which may answer with anti-dumping duties.'),
 ('Which economist is best known for the theory of comparative advantage?', 'David Ricardo', ['Adam Smith', 'John Maynard Keynes', 'Karl Marx'], 'David Ricardo set out comparative advantage in 1817: countries gain by specialising in what they produce at the lowest opportunity cost. Adam Smith wrote about absolute advantage.'),
 ('If a country devalues its currency, its exports usually become:', 'Cheaper for foreign buyers', ['Dearer for foreign buyers', 'Subject to more export duty', 'Harder to transport'], 'After devaluation, foreigners need less of their own currency to buy the same goods, so exports become cheaper and imports dearer.'),
 ('The direct exchange of goods for other goods, without the use of money, is called:', 'Barter', ['Credit sale', 'Hire purchase', 'Retail trade'], 'Barter swaps goods directly. Its weakness is the need for a double coincidence of wants, which money removes.'),
 ('Customs duty is an example of:', 'An indirect tax', ['A direct tax', 'A property tax', 'A capital gains tax'], 'Customs duty is paid by the importer but passed on in the price, so the final buyer bears it indirectly. Income tax is a direct tax.'),
 ('Importing goods in order to export them again, often after storage or repacking, is called:', 'Entrepôt trade', ['Barter trade', 'Retail trade', 'Internal trade'], 'Entrepôt trade is re-export trade through a port or centre. Singapore and Dubai grew rich on it.'),
 ('Goods made for export inside a free trade zone are usually:', 'Free of customs duty', ['Taxed at double rates', 'Sold only at home', 'Banned from export'], 'Free trade zones let firms import inputs and export products without paying the normal customs duties, to attract investment and exports.'),
]
